hi there steve kaufman here um i'm going  to uh focus in this uh video  on um  a question that comes up all the time  and that is  you know don't you forget your by the  way this is london school of economics  my son went there so i got a t-shirt  from him  um  the question is you know don't you  forget your languages  or how do you maintain your languages  that's a very common question and i'm  going to get into that but first i want  to address another question that i get  all the time and that is  you know i'm very interested in  languages i want to do a job i want to  have a job which will involve me with  languages or things of that nature what  do you recommend  so let's deal with that second question  first because i can deal with it very  quickly in my experience the more  languages you speak the more  opportunities you have the more  connections you can make the more  opportunities are going to come your way  however  i'm not aware of many jobs where you can  go and say look at me i speak all these  languages  please give me a job generally speaking  the language skill is a secondary it's a  it's something that you kind of keep  uh in the background that's going to  help you do better that's going to help  you connect with more people it's going  to make it more likely that you'll find  a job or that you'll have opportunities  in your business but i always take in  the position that it's not something  that you lead with it's not something  that's going to necessarily get you the  job it's almost like an added bonus that  the employer  gets or it's an addis added the trump  card that you have that's going to help  you succeed  you have to have something else  whether you're interested in doing  business whether you're interested in  music whether you're interested in  whatever it might be you need to develop  that other skill i don't think unless  you want to be an interpreter  or a translator or something that's very  specifically or a language teacher uh  the language is just a tremendous bonus  that you have in addition to what has to  be your main skill or  even personality traits like being  thorough and and doing what you say  you're going to do and and being  hard-working and and giving more than  you take and all of these other good  things that i think  enable people to build up  the goodwill that leads to success  okay now let's get on to the subject of  forgetting languages don't you forget  languages how do you not forget  languages the simple answer here is i  don't forget languages i have not  forgotten a language  and i think there are several reasons  for this  first of all  my method of learning is massive input  if you look at my statistics  on check i should have looked them up  but i've probably read the equivalent of  i don't know how many books  and everything that i've read i've  listened to  um  you know i don't know how many it's got  to be five six ten books worth of check  that i've listened to  in the last nine months or so that i've  read and listened to and saved words and  so so i'm i'm exposing my brain to this  massive  deluge of content and and this is  building a solid base in the language if  i were simply trying to study word lists  or trying to memorize the clension  tables  or learn grammar rules i i think those  things you can forget those are the  kinds of things that you remember for a  test uh but very quickly forget i've had  the experience myself i've studied  say russian or czech you know declension  tables uh for nouns  and i think i have them for a day or two  and then they're gone again  however uh phrases that i've heard uh  many times and words that i've seen many  times  these combination of words with the noun  and the right ending  combined with a certain preposition  uh all of these things start to become  part of  you know strong you know patterns that  are built in the brain and we don't  forget them  so  the first answer is if you don't want to  forget languages learn them the hard way  if you want in other words invest the  time  put the time into enough exposure to the  language that you are building these  sediments  the these this accumulated experience  with the language in your brain  the second thing is  i  am not bothered by the fact that when i  start up in the language that i haven't  spoken for a while that i'm rusty that  doesn't bother me at all  in fact i'm going to do another video on  the importance of low expectations  i think people who expect too much of  themselves end up being frustrated  lose confidence  you know lose interest whatever  i mean if i haven't spoken swedish or  spanish or something for a while i'm  going to stumble when i start up again  and sometimes people come at me on  youtube and oh you don't speak islam is  very well you made this other mistake  and stuff yeah sure whatever it does  bother me  uh i know that if i were then to be put  in a situation for a day or two or three  where i had to use that language  regularly and i was once again listening  to it all the time and everything that  was in my brain was starting to revive  that i would do fine  in fact i've had the experience that and  i've mentioned this before that if i  leave a language and study some other  language when i come back to that first  language that i left i'm actually better  in the language because my overall  language learning and language using  capability has improved by learning  other languages and the fact that i  stumble out of the gate so to speak  for the first day or two  so what  i have no particular expectations i know  that in any language  you know a second third fourth fifth  language that you speak you'll do better  on some days than on other days  and that's what it is and some days it's  tiring and some days  it's frustrating to try and look for  words that you can't find and you get  annoyed and you would like to retreat  back into your native language and other  days you're just  walking on water and doing very well and  so whichever happens to be the case that  day that's good enough for me i don't  don't go in there with any particular  expectations  so and when i hear people say oh yes you  know my father came over from the old  country and he hasn't speaking spoken  polish or italian in 50 years and now he  can't speak it  i kind of tend to take that with a grain  of salt i think if that particular  father were  set back into a polish italian  portuguese chinese whatever environment  that that language would come back to  him if he'd been exposed to it for 10 or  20 or 30 years as a young person and  then had been away from it for 30 40  years when he or she goes back to that  environment it'll come right back  very very quickly so to me  i don't forget languages  how do i maintain them if i quickly if i  need to  get up to speed like sometimes i'll be  asked to appear on a mandarin chinese  television program here in vancouver  i'll get out some audio books some cds  i'll listen to them i'll do a better  reading give myself some exposure  one thing i certainly don't look at is  grammar books  if you want to get yourself back to  speed you need more exposure let's not  say i don't look at grammar books i will  occasionally look at grammar books  thumb through them so to speak but  to get yourself back up to speed  you need a lot of exposure and i've been  away from it for a while the first night  out with native speakers you might  stumble a bit but you're  very quickly right back in the groove at  least that has been my experience so  there you have it on how not to forget  or how to maintain your languages thanks  for listening
 

